<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Entropy Plots</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Plots for the mean entropy for subthough answer distribution for AIME2024 and AIME2025 are shown in Figure <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Appendix A Entropy Plots ‣ Beyond the Last Answer: Your Reasoning Trace Uncovers More than You Think" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#A1.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix A Entropy Plots ‣ Beyond the Last Answer: Your Reasoning Trace Uncovers More than You Think" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. We observed in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Answer Distribution Entropy and Correctness ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Beyond the Last Answer: Your Reasoning Trace Uncovers More than You Think" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, the mean entropy for correctly solved problems is always lower than those of incorrectly solved problems highlighing how models tend to give a diverse selection of answers per subthought for questions they are having hard time solving correctly.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="A1.F5.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2504.20708v1/entropy_comparison_correct_vs_incorrect_QwQ-32B_aime_2024_greedy.png" id="A1.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/381;" width="476" height="381" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
